pipe.geo
```geo
// pipe.geo - Gmsh geometry for structured hexahedral pipe mesh
// Generates 32000 linear hex elements (Nx=100, Nr=16, Ntheta=20)
// for spectral element order 7 (lx1=8) in NekRS

SetFactory("OpenCASCADE");

R = 1.0;
L = 2*Pi;
Nx = 100;
Nr = 16;
Ntheta = 20;
Nq = Ntheta/4; // 5 elements per quadrant in theta

// Quadrant 1 (y>=0, z>=0) in y-z plane at x=0
Point(1) = {0, 0, 0, 1.0};
Point(2) = {0, R, 0, 1.0};
Point(3) = {0, 0, R, 1.0};
Point(4) = {0, R/sqrt(2), R/sqrt(2), 1.0};

Circle(1) = {2, 1, 4};
Circle(2) = {4, 1, 3};
Line(3) = {1, 2};
Line(4) = {1, 3};

Curve Loop(1) = {3, 1, -2, 4};
Plane Surface(1) = {1};

Transfinite Curve {3, 4} = Nr+1 Using Progression 1;
Transfinite Curve {1, 2} = Nq+1 Using Progression 1;
Transfinite Surface {1} = {1,2,4,3};
Recombine Surface {1};

// Create other three quadrants by rotation around x-axis
Rotate {{0,0,0}, {1,0,0}, Pi/2} { Duplicata{ Surface{1}; } }
Rotate {{0,0,0}, {1,0,0}, Pi}   { Duplicata{ Surface{1}; } }
Rotate {{0,0,0}, {1,0,0}, 3*Pi/2} { Duplicata{ Surface{1}; } }

// Extrude all four surfaces along x to form the pipe volume
Extrude {L, 0, 0} {
  Surface{1, 2, 3, 4}; Layers{Nx}; Recombine;
}

// Physical groups for boundary conditions and volume
Physical Surface("inlet") = {5, 9, 13, 17};   // x=0 faces (IDs may vary)
Physical Surface("outlet") = {6, 10, 14, 18}; // x=L faces
Physical Surface("wall") = {7,8, 11,12, 15,16, 19,20}; // cylindrical wall
Physical Volume("fluid") = {1,2,3,4};         // interior volumes

// Mesh settings
Mesh.ElementOrder = 1;
Mesh.RecombineAll = 1;
Mesh.Algorithm = 8;      // 2D: Frontal-Delaunay for quads
Mesh.Algorithm3D = 1;    // 3D: Delaunay
Mesh.SaveAll = 1;
```
This Gmsh geometry file describes the pipe domain and the structured hexahedral mesh (100×16×20 = 32,000 elements) that will be converted to the binary spectral-element mesh required by NekRS.

Mesh generation commands (not a file, but required to produce the binary mesh)
```
gmsh -3 pipe.geo -o pipe.msh
gmsh2nek pipe
nek2to3 pipe
```
These commands generate the binary NekRS mesh file `pipe.nrs` from the Gmsh description: first creating a linear Gmsh mesh (`pipe.msh`), then converting to Nek5000 format (`pipe.re2`, `pipe.ma2`), and finally to the NekRS binary format (`pipe.nrs`). The binary mesh itself is not a text input file and is produced at runtime by this toolchain.
